## The problem

Let's say there exists some code like:
```
function   doSomething () {
     var  arr = [];
    // ...
}
//...
doSomething ();
var  result;
result = doSomething ();  
console .log(result);
``
The above example shows us three things:

1- We have defined a new variable called "arr" inside our function
2- Inside said function, we've assigned something into "arr"
3- After executing such a function, we're trying to access its return value from outside by using same name ("arr")

Now let's try to understand why accessing variables with names they were declared under might not work properly anymore...

### What happened so far?

When functions get executed, all local variables within them go out of scope until next execution or end of current script run. In case if those locals got reassigned during runtime, then only latest assignment gets preserved after closing corresponding curly-bracket block.

So now consider following instead:
```
( function ) {

         var  x ;
        console.log(x);  

}();
x=5; 
(function)();
console.log(x);// undefined
``
Here even though we tried to log last known state of "x", but since no assignments exist on closure-block level, JS engine simply says: "I don't know".

